/* ===== memory that behaves like a mind =====
   The old rule kept each person's twelve newest memories and dropped the rest, so a betrayal was lost as soon as twelve trivial things had happened after it.
   Now a memory fades the way a real one does: by time, slowed by how much it mattered (emotional memories last, a wound lasts longest), by being recalled (each recall, spaced out, makes it last longer), and by sleep, which replays what mattered.
